    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.078.c2",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "explain",
      "headline": "Coins with a message",
      "body": "The denarius, a silver coin, was the standard Roman coin for centuries. Coins carried the emperor’s portrait and short messages, spreading his image across the empire. Over time emperors reduced the silver in the denarius, and by the 3rd century CE it held very little."
    },
    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.078.c3",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "explain",
      "headline": "Goods on the move",
      "body": "Wine, olive oil and fish sauce moved around the Mediterranean in amphorae, clay jars made for shipping. Roman traders sailed to India, and Chinese silk reached Rome through many middlemen along the Silk Road.",
      "callout": "Amphora: a two-handled shipping jar"
    },
    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.078.c4",
      "type": "fact",
      "fact": "Monte Testaccio is an artificial hill.",
      "context": "It is made of fragments of millions of broken olive oil amphorae, dumped in Rome after their oil was used."
    },
